            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Areas of your home that have some air leakage are common spots such as external outlets and light switches, the vent located above the propane fireplace, and from your crawlspace. The crawlspace could be a bit more difficult to reduce air leakage in compared to the other areas, but this could result in good energy savings.



Air sealing  

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000  is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved.  This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake. 

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve  or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets  defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing  professional. 

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>As you mentioned your interest in adding insulation to the external side of your main walls, I have modeled them with an increase of R20 (doubling the value of the 2x6 portion of the wall). This would result in some energy savings for you, however considering your walls are already fairly thick (2x6 with insulated strapping) you would see less benefit compared to an existing 2x6 wall with no other insulation. I would recommend looking into adding some rigid foam insulation with more focus being on creating a new and improved air barrier on the exterior. By tightening the home, less heat will escape resulting in higher energy efficiency than just through added insulation. This would coincide well with increasing the air tightness of the crawlspace and would work well with replacing your existing siding. 



Exterior wall insulation 

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is  available for adding insulation to the exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are  based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the  amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home,  excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant. 

You are eligible for a Good Energy insulation rebate if you add R20 or more to exterior  walls. The rebate value is $2.50 per square foot for the addition of R20 to R29  insulation and $3.50 per square foot for the addition of R30 insulation or greater. 

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>Your attic ceiling in the center of the home has limited access, but was assessed at R39.93. Although there would be some savings by upping the roof to an R60, the benefit for energy savings compared to other retrofits would be low. I would recommend focusing funds elsewhere before adding insulation to the roof. 



However, from the images I took it appears the insulation varies in height throughout the roof. Redistributing the insulation could be a good idea, especially if you were already up there for other work.



Due to your current insulation level your roof is uneligable for Canada Greener Homes Grant. However, you are eligible for a Good Energy insulation rebate through the Yukon Government if you add R20 or more to your  ceiling. The rebate value is $3.50 per square foot for the addition of R30 insulation or  greater and $2.50 per square foot for the addition of R20 to R29 insulation. The rebate value is determined by the lowest amount of insulation added (ie. at the heels in an attic ceiling). 



</ceilings>
                <ceilingsCathedral>Your cathedral ceiling was assessed at R38.25. Similar to your gable ceiling, although there would be some savings by upping the roof to an R60, the benefit for energy savings compared to other retrofits would be low. I would recommend focusing funds elsewhere before adding insulation to the roof. 



Due to your current insulation level your roof is uneligable for Canada Greener Homes Grant. However, you are eligible for a Good Energy insulation rebate through the Yukon Government if you add R20 or more to your  ceiling. The rebate value is $3.50 per square foot for the addition of R30 insulation or  greater and $2.50 per square foot for the addition of R20 to R29 insulation. The rebate value is determined by the lowest amount of insulation added (ie. at the heels in an attic ceiling). 



</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors>Although you have a small exposed floor and may not see any large energy savings from upgrading it, it may help with comfort levels if you find those rooms slightly cool. 



Exposed Floor Insulation 

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $1500 is available  for adding insulation to the entire exposed floors. Insulating exposed floors may result  in energy savings and improve comfort in rooms above exposed floors. An example of  an exposed floor is the floor of a house on piers. To be eligible for a grant, the entire  exposed floor area must be insulated to reach more than RSI 8.81 (R-50) with a  minimum RSI 1.76 (R-10) of the insulation being continuous. 

</exposedFloors>
                <foundation>Adding insulation to both of your foundations could result in some good energy savings. There would also be the potential to increase the air tightness of the home, more specifically in the crawlspace. However, due to the added cost of excavating around the home to add insulation on the exterior side, the cost to benefit ratio will be more skewed than doing the same on the main floor walls.



The Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative offers grants of up to $1,500 for adding insulation to basement walls. Insulating basement walls can reduce energy costs and improve comfort. Grants are based on the RSI/R-value of the added insulation and the percentage of wall area insulated.The current amount of insulation does not affect the grant amounts. A minimum of 20% of the

exterior foundation wall area must be insulated.



You are eligible for a Good Energy insulation rebate if you add R20 or more to exterior  walls. The rebate value is $2.50 per square foot for the addition of R20 to R29  insulation and $3.50 per square foot for the addition of R30 insulation or greater. 



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, a grant of $875 is available for  waterproofing exterior foundation walls. This resiliency measure must be combined  with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant to be eligible. 



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, a grant of $600 is available for moisture  proofing 100% of the crawl space floor, walls and headers. This resiliency measure  must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes  Grant to be eligible.

</foundation>
                <windows>The majority of your windows are already in great condition and are of good energy efficiency. You could replace the 3 existing double pane windows along with the 2 skylights to triple pane low e units as modelled here. 



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per  window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY  STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window.  Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products  can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility  of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of  eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. 



You are also eligible for an additional $100 per window through the Good Energy window rebate. </windows>
                <VentilationSystem>If you are to add any type of air sealing to the home, I would highly recommend adding an HRV unit. I would also suggest speaking with a Radon specialist with regards to their recommendations on bringing in new fresh air to a tighter building envelope. 



You may be eligable for Yukon specific rebates when installing a HRV system. Visit  https://yukon.ca/en/buildings-and-homes for more details.</VentilationSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>A cold climate air source heat pump could be a good option in your home, specifically a mini split system. This would allow you to strategically place multiple heating heads in the different areas of the home, and could replace your existing baseboard heaters.



Depending upon the region in which you live and how you heat your home today, the  installation of a heat pump in your home may result in higher utility costs. Consult with an HVAC professional in this field to determine if this type of system is practical and  the right choice for your circumstances. 

To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is  included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant  webpage. 

Your  new heat pump must be sized to supply the amount of heat required for each room or,  in the case of a centrally ducted system or small home, for the entire home. Ask your  heating professional to size the heat pump to meet these specific heating loads. 

For more information: Natural Resources Canada has developed a package of materials  related to air source heat pump sizing and selection, intended for use by mechanical  system designers and renovation contractors. https://www.nrcan.gc.ca/maps-tools and-publications/tools/modelling-tools/toolkit-for-air-source-heat-pump-sizing-and selection/23558. 



You are also eligible for a Good Energy rebate of 40% of project costs, up to a  maximum of $8,000 for the installation of a ducted system or mini- or multi-split air  source heat pump system. Rebates are based on equipment costs, labour, electrical  upgrades, construction costs and/or contracting and professional expenses related to  the installation and supporting infrastructure.  

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>Your house and property could be a great candidate for a small solar system, especially with the option of having a ground mounted system. 



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,000 per KW (up to a max of $5000) is  available for installing a photovoltaic solar system. If you choose to install a photovoltaic  (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer is undertaken to provide detailed information on  considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the  size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV system must have peak  power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be installed under a Territorial electrical permit and must pass inspection.  

Adding a solar PV system to your home would allow for onsite self-generation, which would reduce home electrical bills by offsetting your consumption from the grid, and by  potentially earning annual reimbursement from export to the grid via the Micro generation Program. You would be also eligible for the Good Energy rebate of $800 per  kW of generating capacity to a maximum of $5,000 per system per year. 

For every 1.0 kW DC of array generating capacity installed in this region, a Yukoner can  expect approximately 1,000 kWh of onsite generation annually. A 7.5 kW solar PV  array would generate approximately 7,500 kWh/yr and provide approximately $1,425  in annual offset savings and reimbursement earnings through the MicroGeneration  Program.

</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT 

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for  the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the  implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020. 

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home  retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for  complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation  and guidelines. 

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION 

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the  recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes  Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit. 

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain  conditions for eligibility. These include: 

Â In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at  least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in  this Renovation Upgrade Report 

Â If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must  also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a  grant

Â Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility  criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener  Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner  Information Kit 

Â All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must  be installed by a licensed and trained professional 

Â It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the  Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal  (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that: 

o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a  trained and licensed professional* 

o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance  with CSA standards 

o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home 

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you  obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or  territory. 

Â All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if  they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada. 

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES 

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant  with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding  you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and  post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure. 

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. 

Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include:  

Â Good Energy rebates 

Â Microgeneration program 

Â Canada Greener Homes loans.

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
